Subject: AOT: engine helpers for the constant pool and pure binary operations of an inlined callee, taking the callee's bytecode

What this does
  - `qjs_aot_load_const_b(ctx, b, kind, idx, &out)` and `qjs_aot_array_const_b(ctx, b, idx, &out)` do `load_const` and `array_const` from the bytecode `b` instead of a frame (the `_b` forms `qjs_aot_object_template_b` and `qjs_aot_object_fill_b` already exist).
  - `qjs_aot_binary_pure(ctx, sub, a, b, &out)` and `qjs_aot_binary_cpool_pure(ctx, b, sub, idx, kind, a, &out)` run a binary operation whose operands are numbers or primitives that convert without running code (and a short string `+` string or int): 1 with an owned result, 0 when the operands are anything else (an object, a symbol, a BigInt, a long string, `instanceof`/`in`), -1 on out of memory. The right operand of the second comes from `b`'s pool (kind 0) or an atom.
  - Declarations in quickjs-aot-rt.h.

Why we need it
  F53 (octane regexp: the AOT binary crashed with SIGSEGV). The generator lowered the engine calls of an inlined callee's allocating and pool operations (`binary_cpool`, `load_const`, `array_const`, `object_template`, `object_fill`) with `f`, the ROOT function: the callee's pool index was read from the root's pool, a wrong constant or, in a root without a pool, a null read. The helpers above let the copy name its callee's bytecode (from the closure its guard proved). The pure forms leave nothing half done: a copy has no engine step behind it, so an operation that could run user code (valueOf, a getter) or throw leaves the copy through its exit before the instruction, and the interpreter does it.

Soundness
  The helpers read `b->cpool[idx]` for an index the compiler produced for that function; a copy calls them only with the bytecode of the callee its guard proved by identity (or by identical code, whose pool has the same values). They allocate only the result.
--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
@@ -69333,0 +69334,16 @@
+}
+
+/* The three forms below are for the code of an INLINED CALLEE (a copy): the copy has the callee's bytecode `b`
+   (bound from the closure its guard proved) and no frame of its own to give the helpers above, whose `f` is the
+   root's. Same operations, from `b`'s pool and without the in-place append (that needs the frame). */
+int qjs_aot_load_const_b(JSContext *ctx, JSFunctionBytecode *b, int kind, uint32_t idx,
+                         JSValue *out)
+{
+    *out = kind == REG_CONST_CPOOL ? js_dup(b->cpool[idx]) : JS_AtomToValue(ctx, idx);
+    return JS_IsException(*out) ? -1 : 0;
+}
+
+int qjs_aot_array_const_b(JSContext *ctx, JSFunctionBytecode *b, uint32_t idx, JSValue *out)
+{
+    *out = regvm_array_from_const(ctx, b->cpool[idx]);
+    return JS_IsException(*out) ? -1 : 0;
@@ -69335,0 +69352,37 @@
+/* `a <sub> b` for a copy: only the cases that run no user code and cannot throw but for out of memory (numbers,
+   a short string with a string or an int). 1 with an owned *out, 0 when it is anything else (the copy then leaves
+   at the instruction, before any effect), -1 on out of memory. */
+int qjs_aot_binary_pure(JSContext *ctx, int sub, JSValue a, JSValue b, JSValue *out)
+{
+    int ta = JS_VALUE_GET_TAG(a), tb = JS_VALUE_GET_TAG(b);
+
+    if (regvm_num_fast(sub, a, b, out))
+        return 1;
+    /* numbers and the primitives that convert without running code */
+    if (sub != REG_BIN_INSTANCEOF && sub != REG_BIN_IN && sub != REG_BIN_PRIVATE_IN &&
+        ((uint32_t)ta <= JS_TAG_UNDEFINED || ta == JS_TAG_FLOAT64)) {
+        if ((uint32_t)tb <= JS_TAG_UNDEFINED || tb == JS_TAG_FLOAT64)
+            return js_regvm_binary(ctx, sub, a, b, out) ? -1 : 1;
+    }
+    if (sub == REG_BIN_ADD)
+        return js_concat_short(ctx, a, b, out);
+    return 0;
+}
+
+/* The same with the right operand from the callee's pool (`kind` REG_CONST_CPOOL) or an atom. */
+int qjs_aot_binary_cpool_pure(JSContext *ctx, JSFunctionBytecode *b, int sub, uint32_t idx,
+                              int kind, JSValue a, JSValue *out)
+{
+    JSValue cst;
+    int r;
+
+    if (kind == REG_CONST_CPOOL)
+        return qjs_aot_binary_pure(ctx, sub, a, b->cpool[idx], out);
+    cst = JS_AtomToValue(ctx, (JSAtom)idx);
+    if (JS_IsException(cst))
+        return -1;
+    r = qjs_aot_binary_pure(ctx, sub, a, cst, out);
+    JS_FreeValue(ctx, cst);
+    return r;
+}
+
--- a/quickjs-aot-rt.h
+++ b/quickjs-aot-rt.h
@@ -614,0 +615,8 @@
+/* The same, array_const and the pure binary forms with the bytecode of an inlined callee instead of the root's frame
+   (see quickjs.c). The binary forms return 1 with an owned *out, 0 when the operands are not a case that runs no user
+   code (the copy leaves at the instruction), -1 on out of memory. */
+int qjs_aot_load_const_b(JSContext *ctx, struct JSFunctionBytecode *b, int kind, uint32_t idx, JSValue *out);
+int qjs_aot_array_const_b(JSContext *ctx, struct JSFunctionBytecode *b, uint32_t idx, JSValue *out);
+int qjs_aot_binary_pure(JSContext *ctx, int sub, JSValue a, JSValue b, JSValue *out);
+int qjs_aot_binary_cpool_pure(JSContext *ctx, struct JSFunctionBytecode *b, int sub, uint32_t idx, int kind,
+                              JSValue a, JSValue *out);
